The main idea is much simpler if we think about what the calculator did
to each base-B digit.

Suppose the real base-B representation of X is:
d1 d2 d3 ...

where every digit satisfies:
0 <= d[i] < B

The calculator displays each digit as:
- 0...9 -> one character, for example 7 -> "7"
- 10...B-1 -> its decimal representation, for example 14 -> "14"

So the displayed string Y is actually a concatenation of decimal representation of the base-B digits.

